API Architecture Document — TeamTamApi
1. Purpose
Describe how requests flow through the system, responsibilities of each layer, and operational/security considerations for TeamTamApi (.NET 6).
2. High-level summary
•	ASP.NET Core (.NET 6) Web API using the minimal host pattern (Program.cs).
•	Attribute-routed controllers (versioned routes under api/v1/).
•	Thin controllers that validate requests and delegate to the service layer (e.g., AdminDashboardController → IDailyReportService).
•	MongoDB as primary datastore; many repository implementations under TeamTamApi.DataAccess.Repositories.
•	JWT Bearer authentication with role-based authorization using RoleConstant.
•	Additional per-request middleware: organization context and auth validation.
•	Extensive DI registration in Program.cs for services, repositories, cache providers and integrations.
3. Components and responsibilities
•	Client
•	Web / mobile / integration clients call REST endpoints.
•	Ingress / Load Balancer
•	TLS termination, routing to API instances.
•	Routing & Endpoint metadata
•	__UseRouting__ sets up endpoint metadata used by subsequent middleware.
•	Authentication
•	JWT Bearer configured in Program.cs. Signing key from AppSettingsConfig (Azure Key Vault used for secret management).
•	Authorization
•	Role-based [Authorize(Roles = ...)] on controllers/actions (coarse-grained).
•	Middleware
•	OrganizationDbMiddleware — populates organization context (runs after authentication so User is available).
•	AuthValidationMiddleware — additional API-key / permission validations.
•	AppSettingConfigMiddleware — loads/validates application settings per request.
•	Controllers
•	Marked [ApiController] and route-prefixed (e.g., api/v1/AdminDashboard).
•	Validate incoming DTOs and User organization membership via extension helpers (e.g., User.ValidateOrganizationId(...)) before calling services.
•	Service layer
•	Business logic; interfaces under TeamTamApi.Service.Interfaces and implementations under TeamTamApi.Service.Services.
•	Responsible for transaction semantics, cross-service orchestration, caching invocation, and input validation (beyond controller checks).
•	Repository/data access
•	MongoDB repositories and data mapping logic under TeamTamApi.DataAccess.
•	BSON serializers registered for Guid, DateTime, DateTimeOffset.
•	Caching
•	IMemoryCache plus domain cache providers (e.g., IUserCacheProvider, IRoleCacheProvider) for hot data.
•	External integrations
•	Azure Key Vault (secrets), Azure Service Bus, payment gateways (PayU, Razorpay), cloud storage (AWS/Azure), PDF generation (DinkToPdf), SignalR, etc.
•	Observability
•	Serilog configured to write to Console and MongoDB collection log. UseSerilogRequestLogging() enabled.
•	API docs
•	Swagger/OpenAPI with two docs: internal (routes under api/v1/) and public (api/integration/v1/).
•	Security definitions for X-Api-Key and Bearer JWT.
4. Request / response flow (typical)
1.	Client sends HTTPS request to Load Balancer / Ingress.
2.	Request hits API instance; TLS handled at ingress.
3.	ASP.NET Core pipeline: __UseRouting__ -> __UseAuthentication__ -> __UseAuthorization__.
4.	After auth, custom middlewares run: OrganizationDbMiddleware, AuthValidationMiddleware, AppSettingConfigMiddleware.
5.	Controller selected and executed (model binding + validation handled by [ApiController]).
6.	Controller authorizes that User belongs to requested organization (via User.ValidateOrganizationId*).
7.	Controller calls service layer interface (injected via DI).
8.	Service coordinates domain processing, may call repositories, cache providers, or external services.
9.	Repository accesses MongoDB; services may update caches.
10.	Service returns DTO → controller returns HTTP response (serialized JSON).
5. Middleware ordering (important)
The pipeline in Program.cs enforces this order:
•	UseRouting
•	UseAuthentication
•	UseAuthorization
•	OrganizationDbMiddleware (needs authenticated User)
•	AuthValidationMiddleware
•	AppSettingConfigMiddleware
•	MapControllers
•	UseSerilogRequestLogging
Correct ordering is required so that middleware that relies on HttpContext.User runs after authentication.
6. Dependency injection and lifetimes
•	Most services and repositories are registered as Scoped in Program.cs.
•	Singletons for shared clients/resources:
•	IConverter (DinkToPdf) as singleton.
•	ServiceBusClient as singleton.
•	IWebhookSignatureService as singleton.
•	Use IHttpClientFactory (already called with AddHttpClient()) for external HTTP callers.
7. Security considerations
•	JWT configuration currently sets ValidateIssuer = false and ValidateAudience = false — consider enabling issuer/audience checks when tokens are issued under known authority.
•	Secrets are sourced from Azure Key Vault (good).
•	Swagger protected by configuration; ensure Swagger UI not exposed in unintended environments.
•	Validate and rate-limit API key usage for integration endpoints.
•	Ensure controllers use User.ValidateOrganizationId* consistently to prevent horizontal privilege escalation.
8. Observability & ops
•	Serilog logs to MongoDB for errors, console for all.
•	Consider adding structured correlation IDs and logging middleware to trace requests end-to-end.
•	Health checks not present in Program.cs—add AddHealthChecks() and endpoints for orchestrators.
9. Scalability & deployment
•	App is horizontally scalable behind LB; keep DI-registered singletons thread-safe.
•	Use Azure Service Bus for async/background work; process with worker services if needed.
•	External integrations should have retry/backoff (use Polly with IHttpClientFactory).
10. Extensibility & recommended improvements
•	Add a global exception-handling middleware (consistent error shape).
•	Add OpenTelemetry or Application Insights for traces and metrics.
•	Centralize role and permission checks: augment role-based attributes with a fine-grained permission service (already present: IApiPermissionService).
•	Add API versioning package if multiple vX routes evolve beyond attribute versioning.
•	Harden JWT validation (issuer/audience) and rotate signing keys using Key Vault/Credential rotation patterns.
11. Operational runbook (quick)
•	Config/Secrets: stored in Azure Key Vault; Program.cs populates AppSettingsConfig.
•	Logs: Serilog -> MongoDB log collection (errors) and console (all).
•	CORS: resolved at startup by ICorsConfigurationService reading allowed origins from DB.
•	Restart: stateless API, safe to scale/restart; ensure background queues processed by Workers.
